SUNDARAKANDA - Sarga 1
vanara
vayu - Rama and vayu

BALA KANDA - Sarga 1
- Verse 38: At Nandigrāma, Bharata placed Rāma's sacred sandals (pādukās) upon the throne as the sovereign proxy (nyāsa) of Rāma, administering the kingdom purely as their custodian.
- Verse 40: Rāma left Citrakūṭa and entered the Daṇḍaka forest because repeated visits from the citizens and royal family of Ayodhyā would disrupt the peace and penance of the local munis and r̥ṣis.
- Verse 46: The condensed śloka states that Śūrpaṇakhā was disfigured by Rāma (tēna virūpitā); the full narrative in Araṇya Kāṇḍa explains that Lakṣmaṇa cut off her nose and ears on Rāma's command after she lunged to attack Sītā.
- Verse 76: Although Śloka 75 only mentions capture in general terms (grahaṇaṁ samupāgamat), Indrajit had bound Hanumān using the Brahmāstra (astrēṇa). By Lord Brahmā's boon, the divine weapon released its hold the moment the Rākṣasas tied him with physical ropes (yantriṇaḥ). Hanumān willingly endured being bound so that he could be brought directly into Rāvaṇa's court.

BALA KANDA - Sarga 2
- Verse 15: Classical commentators explain that the spontaneous verse "Mā niṣāda..." is simultaneously an esoteric benediction (maṅgala-śloka) addressed to Śrīman Nārāyaṇa (Mā-niṣāda: the abode of Goddess Lakṣmī) that contains the seed (bīja) of all seven Kāṇḍas of the Rāmāyaṇa:
  1. Bāla Kāṇḍa ("Mā-niṣāda"): The incarnation and wedding of Rāma with Sītā (Lakṣmī).
  2. Ayodhyā Kāṇḍa ("pratiṣṭhāṁ tvam agamaḥ"): Renouncing the established throne to depart into forest exile.
  3. Araṇya Kāṇḍa ("mithunāt ekam"): The tragic separation of the divine couple through Sītā's abduction.
  4. Kiṣkindhā Kāṇḍa ("mithunād ekam avadhīḥ"): Slaying one of the two brothers (Vāli of the Vāli-Sugrīva pair).
  5. Sundara Kāṇḍa ("kāma-mōhitam"): Discovering Sītā held in captivity by the lust-maddened Rāvaṇa.
  6. Yuddha Kāṇḍa ("avadhīḥ"): Slaying Rāvaṇa in the great battle and rescuing Sītā.
  7. Uttara Kāṇḍa ("śāśvatīḥ samāḥ pratiṣṭhām"): Establishing eternal righteous rule (Rāma-rājya) for thousands of years and attaining everlasting glory.


BALA KANDA - Sarga 9
- Verse 5: The "twofold brahmacarya" (dvaividhyaṁ brahmacaryasya) recognized in the śāstras refers to two distinct disciplines:
  1. Celibacy of studentship (Upakurvāṇa): Total celibacy observed during the years of Vedic study under a guru prior to marriage.
  2. Celibacy of the householder (Gṛhastha-brahmacarya): The restraint practiced after marriage, wherein marital intimacy is restricted solely to the times prescribed by the śāstras, observing complete celibacy on all other days.
- Verse 15: Classical commentators explain that "dreading the ṛṣi" (r̥ṣērbhītāḥ) specifically denotes fear of Sage Vibhāṇḍaka's fierce anger and curse. The ministers and priests were terrified of venturing into the hermitage to take his only son, as Vibhāṇḍaka possessed formidable ascetic power and was fiercely protective of Ṛśyaśṛṅga's secluded life.
- Verse 17-18: Classical commentators clarify Princess Śāntā's parentage and why Ṛśyaśṛṅga is called Daśaratha's son-in-law (jāmātā): Śāntā was biologically the daughter of King Daśaratha (born to Queen Kauśalyā). Because his dear friend King Romapāda of Aṅga was childless, Daśaratha gave Śāntā to him in adoption (dattaka). Romapāda raised her as his own daughter and gave her in marriage to Sage Ṛśyaśṛṅga, making the sage the son-in-law of both kings.

BALA KANDA - Sarga 13
- Verse 31: While the text mentions "many valuable offerings / precious gems" (bahūni ratnāni), classical commentaries explain that the visiting monarchs brought the full customary array of royal gifts befitting an emperor's Aśvamedha: rare gems, pearls, corals, fine silks and fabrics, gold, prized horses and elephants, and regional specialties such as sandalwood and costly aromatics.

BALA KANDA - Sarga 14
- Verse 6: Classical commentators explain that Soma, the "king of plants" (rājā), is the sacred vine whose juice is the chief offering of a Soma yajña. The stalks were soaked in water and then pounded with stones (not squeezed) while the prescribed mantras were chanted. The extracted juice was strained through a woolen filter into a vessel and drawn off in ritual cups, from which it was offered to the devatās, with Indra receiving the first oblation. This extraction and offering is performed three times in a day, forming the three savanas (rites): morning (Prātaḥsavana), midday (Mādhyandina Savana), and evening (Tṛtīyasavana).
- Verse 21: Classical commentators note that while the specification of the interval—the span of two outstretched arms (a vyāma)—is mentioned here alongside these two posts (bāhuvyastaparigrahau), it is illustrative (pradarśanārtham). In Śrauta ritual ordinances, this exact distance separated every adjacent pair among all twenty-one yūpas across the sacrificial ground.
- Verse 23: An aratni is a classical Vedic unit of measure, the cubit—measured from the elbow to the tip of the clenched fist or little finger (approximately 18 inches / 1.5 feet). A height of twenty-one aratnis (around 31.5 feet) for each sacrificial post is prescribed in Śrauta ordinances for the Aśvamedha, matching the number of posts. The phrase 'twenty-one cloths' (vāsobhir ekaviṁśadbhiḥ) refers to the complete set of twenty-one cloths used to drape each of the twenty-one posts individually.
- Verse 24: Classical commentators explain that 'smooth in form' (ślakṣṇarūpasamanvitāḥ) denotes timber of uniform shape that is completely straight and free from crookedness or bends (vakra), hollows or holes (koṭara), knots, and defects. An eight-sided (aṣṭāśri) cross-section is the standard Vedic Śrauta specification for sacrificial posts.
- Verse 25: Classical commentators clarify that 'the Saptarṣis in the sky' (saptarṣayo yathā divi) refers specifically to the Saptarṣi-maṇḍala—the constellation of the Seven Sages (Ursa Major). Adorned with gold (from verse 22), white cloths, garlands, and fragrant pastes, the posts sparkled like bright stars against the sacrificial grounds. Commentators also explain the numerical correspondence: the twenty-one posts were arranged ritually in three heptads (sets of seven), each line evoking the radiant stellar formation of the Saptarṣis.
- Verse 27: The fire altar for the Aśvamedha was a Garuḍa-citi (or Śyena-citi), constructed of fired bricks in the form of a great eagle with outstretched golden wings. In Vedic Śulba geometry, the baseline altar has a measure of six units (puruṣas or aratnis); here it was tripled (triguṇa) to eighteen (aṣṭādaśātmaka), laid out in eighteen successive courses (layers/tiers) of bricks.
- Verse 31: Classical commentators (beginning with early authorities like Kataka) explain that the term viśaśāsa does not indicate that Queen Kausalyā performed the actual physical slaughter. In strict Vedic Śrauta ritual ordinances (as detailed in the Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa), the actual dispatching (saṁjñapana) is the exclusive, designated duty of the Śamitṛ priest. The Queen's prescribed ritual role as Mahiṣī consists of the solemn consecration and tracing of the sacrificial lines (asipatha-kalpanā) using the three sacred blades made of gold, silver, and iron. Because she initiates this vital part of the rite, the action is traditionally attributed to her. Commentators also clarify that her 'supreme joy' (paramayā mudā) was not pleasure in violence, but religious joy at performing the sacred duty that was the direct cause for obtaining a noble son (putra-janma-hetu).
- Verse 32: As the anointed chief queen (Mahiṣī / Paṭṭapu Rāṇi), Kausalyā was ordained by Śrauta ritual to keep the sacred night vigil beside the immolated steed, symbolically absorbing its divine vigor and fertility for the continuity of the solar dynasty. The text emphasizes that she did so with a steady, resolute mind (susthitena cetasā) and purely out of devotion to righteousness (dharmakāmyayā).
- Verse 33: The text designates the three queens by their technical Vedic Śrauta ritual titles: Mahiṣī (the consecrated Chief Queen, Kausalyā), Parivṛktī (the quiet, secondary queen, Sumitrā), and Vāvātā (the beloved, favored queen, Kaikeyī). Classical commentators explain that each of the three chief officiating priests—the Adhvaryu (Yajurveda), Hotā (Ṛgveda), and Udgātā (Sāmaveda)—led one of the queens to touch and hold the consecrated horse while reciting prescribed mantras, ensuring that all three queens shared in the sacred fruit of the sacrifice.
- Verse 36: In major Vedic Śrauta sacrifices such as the Aśvamedha, the ritual college consists of exactly sixteen officiating priests (ṣoḍaśartvijaḥ), comprising four chief priests representing the Vedas and three assistants each: Hotā (with Maitrāvaruṇa, Acchāvāka, Grāvastut); Adhvaryu (with Pratiprasthātā, Neṣṭā, Unnetā); Udgātā (with Prastotā, Pratihartā, Subrahmaṇya); and Brahmā (with Brāhmaṇācchaṁsī, Āgnīdhra, Potā). Together, all sixteen performed the collective oblation of the sacred offerings into the sacrificial fire.
- Verse 40: Classical commentators (Govindarāja and Tilaka) explain that exactly six great subordinate Soma sacrifices (ṣaṭ kratavaḥ) were instituted as auxiliary components of the Aśvamedha: (1) Jyotiṣṭoma, (2) Āyus, (3) Atirātra, (4) Abhijit, (5) Viśvajit, and (6) Aptoryāma. The dual grammatical endings in the verse (such as atirātrau nirmitau) are due to poetic compounding, not two separate Atirātras.
- Verse 52: Classical commentators (Govindarāja and Tilaka) explain the significance of the particle atha: King Daśaratha had already distributed all the vast gold, silver, and wealth from his royal treasury (sarvasve datte sati). When a destitute Brāhmaṇa arrived afterwards seeking alms, the royal coffers were empty; unwilling to let any supplicant leave empty-handed, the king took the precious ornament straight from his own hand (hastābharaṇam) and bestowed it upon him.

BALA KANDA - Sarga 15
- Verse 10: Classical commentators explain that the phrasing 'the sun does not burn hot upon him' (nainaṁ sūryaḥ pratapati) and 'the wind does not blow near him' (pārśvē vāti na mārutaḥ) illustrates how the presiding devatās of nature tremble before Rāvaṇa. Dreading his wrath, Sūrya moderates his fiery intensity and sheds only mild, bearable warmth upon him, Vāyu restrains his violent force to blow only as a gentle, soothing breeze, and Sāgara (the ocean) curbs his rolling waves into stillness whenever Rāvaṇa looks upon him.
- Verse 18: Classical commentators (Govindarāja and Tilaka) explain that the verb niyōkṣyāmahē (literally 'we shall appoint / direct you') functions as prārthayāmahē ('we humbly pray / petition you'). Since the devatās cannot command or assign tasks to the Supreme Being, their address to Śrī Mahāviṣṇu is in the spirit of a reverent supplication.
- Verse 25: The term Tridaśas (literally 'those with three states of life') refers to the celestial devatās (traditionally spoken of as the thirty-three crore devatās). Classical commentators explain that unlike mortals who pass through four stages—childhood (bālya), youth (kaumāra), prime (yauvana), and old age/decay (jarā)—the devatās, having drunk the divine nectar (Amṛta), never suffer wrinkles, senility, or decay. They abide eternally in the third stage of flourishing youth (yauvana), and are thus called Tridaśas ('possessing only three stages').
- Verse 33: Classical commentators explain gatadōṣakalmaṣam (qualifying Svarloka / the celestial realm) as rāga-dveṣābhāvāt: the abode free from the internal defects (dōṣas) of passion/attachment (rāga) and malice/hatred (dveṣa), as well as the sins and impurities (kalmaṣa) born of them.
